     Wi-Fi framework settings for OpenBook PCs: a mac80211 driver (MT7925 on the G14) through wpa_supplicant and
     wificond, with no vendor Wi-Fi HAL (wifi.mk, docs/g14-audio-net.md). -->
<resources>
    <!-- The bands come from the driver through wificond; these let the framework use them. -->
    <bool name="config_wifi5ghzSupport">true</bool>
    <bool name="config_wifi6ghzSupport">true</bool>
    <!-- Changing a station or AP MAC address goes through the vendor HAL, which OpenBook does not have. -->
    <bool name="config_wifi_connected_mac_randomization_supported">false</bool>
    <bool name="config_wifi_p2p_mac_randomization_supported">false</bool>
    <bool name="config_wifi_ap_mac_randomization_supported">false</bool>
    <!-- Screen off and disconnected, the framework scans only through PNO. Without a vendor HAL that means the
         driver's scheduled scan (Nl80211Native.startPnoScan; MT7925 offers one), which this enables; without it
         the G14 logged "PNO is not available" 5 times and then stopped scanning until the screen came on
         (anal178c, docs/g14-audio-net.md). If the scheduled scan fails, the framework retries and gives up as before.
         Only while the system is awake: with no WoWLAN configured, cfg80211 stops scheduled scans (and disconnects)
         at each suspend. -->
    <bool name="config_wifi_background_scan_support">true</bool>
</resources>
